It has long been appreciated that enzymatic catalysis of difficult chemical transformations with high intrinsic activation barricrs requires the prccise positioning of the substrate and the active site residues within a specifically structured protein environment [1]. Our poor understanding of the relationship between a protein's amino acid sequence and its structure, however, impedes the design of proteins with novel catalytic function [2]. Aside from the catalytic function of the “global fold', the primary amino acid sequence includes information for the stability, solubility, supramolecular interactions, cell localization and finally, degradation of the protein. Despite an ever expanding database of structurally characterized natural proteins, which exists as a deciphering aid, the underlying principles for the assembly of enzyme catalytic sites have not yet been resolved.
