## Packing in proteins
Once the mRNA has been translated, a natural prorein folds into its native conformation, which is at the global free energy minimum. Proteins generally possess tightly packed hydrophobic cores in the solid state and in solution [16]. This close packing provides the rigid core necessary for thermodynamic stability and in which can reside domains that possess local flexibility required for conformational changes and catalytic action. Moreover, it is clear that there is sufficient stability in this core to accommodate regions with some degree of plasticity, that is areas tolerant to mutations compensated for by sidechain and backbone reorganization [17]. Thus the intrinsic stability of native proteins yields a general robustness that withstands random mutations as well forms the foundation for catalytic site construction—the major challenge to those designing and synthesizing novel proteins.
Firm control of the packing of non-polar amino acid side chains into the interior of the protein (hydrophobic core packing) over aggregation state (the number of monomer sub-units per coil) and secondary structure has been established. A peptide that preferentially folds into a tightly packed two helix bundle due to the presence of a cavity in the trimeric aggregation state converts into a three helix bundle upon benzene addition [18] which fills the designed cavity. Minor and Kim $[19^{\bullet\bullet}]$ have demonstrated that an 11 amino acid sequence can adopt either an $\mathfrak{a}$ -helical or $\beta$ -sheet secondary structure depending on the hydrophobic packing context. On a larger scale, two reports $[20^{\bullet},21^{\bullet}]$ of transforming predominately $\beta$ -sheet proteins into $\pmb{\alpha}$ -helical proteins by mutation of less than half the total residues illustrates that secondary structure is context-dependent, as it is partly based on local packing arrangements. The reverse transformation ( $\alpha$ -helix to $\upbeta$ -sheet) which should test our understanding of $\upbeta$ -sheet design, however, has not been achieved due to a less devclopcd undcrstanding of $\beta$ -shcet compared to $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix design.
## Design of conformationally specific proteins
The periodic repeat of polar and apolar residues observcd in coiled-coil proteins, (aapolar bpolar Cpolar dapolar eapolar $\mathbf{f_{polar\gpolar}})_{\mathtt{n}}$ ，where $\mathbf{a}{\mathbf{-}}\mathbf{g}$ designate the seven consecutive amino acid side positions within the heptad repeat of the coiled-coil, is adequate to generate helical secondary structure, but, so far, is insuffcient to produce designed proteins with native-like characteristics resulting from well packed hydrophobic cores. Recently, both the minimalist (the rational design of proteins with minimal complexity) and binary patterning (protein design based on the pattern of hydrophobic and hydrophillic amino acids) approaches toward the study of four $\mathfrak{a}$ -helix protein design have shown notable progress toward the clucidation of those factors requisite for the de novo design of well defined, compact, uniquely structured hydrophobic cores.
At least one general rule of protein conformational specificity has been derived from the redesign of the natural RNA-binding repressor of primer (ROP), a homodimeric four- $\pmb{\alpha}$ -helix bundle protein. Mutational studies, de novo design as well as repacking algorithms have provided a series of ROP-like four- $\pmb{\alpha}$ -helix bundles with conformationally specific hydrophobic cores using ‘small versus large' packing arrangements. Each hydrophobic packing layer is composed of two heptad a positions and two heptad $d$ positions, one containing large (leucine or isoleucine) and the other small (alanine or valine) hydrophobes. Repacked mutants of ROP containing hydrophobic layers with small alanines at the $a$ positions and large leucines at the $d$ positions of the coiledcoli heptad repeat (AlazLeuz-8 given in Figure 1) are thermodynamically stable and retain biological activity (that is, RNA affinity), whereas the reverse packing arrangement, large leucines at a and small alanines at $d$ positions, yields a well packed, highly stable protein which lacks RNA-binding capability [25]. Similarly, a design based on first principles provided several peptides with native-like thcrmodynamic behavior. Alanine and valine were incorporated at heptad a positions packing against leucine residues at $d$ positions in both an ROP-like dimcric helix-loop-helix (RLP-3 in Figure 1), which dimerizes to form a four helix bundle, and a four individual helix architecture (which utilizes four single helix monomers to form the four helix bundle) [26,27]. Additionally, a rather symmetric structure (valines or leucines at all a and $d$ positions) derived from ROP by the CORE computer program (designed to optimize hydrophobic packing efficiency) generated a homodimeric four- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{a}$ -helix bundle (HYP-1 in Figure 1) that has native-like characteristics and hyperthermophilic properties [28]. The majority of this experimental evidence suggests that the fabrication of a native-like folded state in synthetic ROPs results from a packing scheme which utilizes small versus large hydrophobes at each packing layer composed of four amino acid sidechains. A related packing scheme with small and large hydrophobes at alternating layers has been employed in the structure-based redesign of an all paralel three helix bundle, Coil- $\mathrm{.V_{\ita}L_{\it d}}$ (so named for the valines at $a$ positions and leucines at $d$ positions) $\left[29^{\bullet},30\right]$ .
