## Dynamics in designed proteins
## Minimalization of proteins
If there is a minimal folding energy, then proteins, natural or designed by the minimalist methodology (often larger than some natural proteins), should be amenable to simplification. In fact, the structure based redesign of a DNA binding zinc finger protein (Zif268) whose 23 amino acids fold into a compact structure without any metal ion supports this assertion $\{35,36^{\bullet\bullet}\}$ . Additionally, Braisted and Wells' $[37^{\bullet\bullet}]$ minimalization of the B protein of protein A which binds the $\mathrm{F_{c}}$ -portion of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Ig}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{Gs}}$ illustrates that protein domains can be effectively simplified since they are not indivisible elements of protein structure. Current research, however, has not fully addressed the consequences of minimizing a designed protein in terms of thermodynamics, function or conformational specificity.
## Cofactor-peptide systems
A requisite step in the design path towards a functional protein (including enzymes) of any size is the incorporation of an active site or cofactors to aid in catalysis. Several model systems containing minimal protein content have been developed to study the fundamentals of protein-cofactor interactions as well as photochemical energy conversion [8], electron transfer [33·] and DNA hydrolysis [9]. These efforts have provided more tractable model systems which are potentially useful as design modules for larger systems of greater complexity.
Utilizing either a self-assembly process or by exploiting solid-phase peptide synthetic chemistry, $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{III})$ and $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ porphyrin-peptide helix-heme-helix complexes have been synthesized as models with future functional potential to the natural heme proteins involved in dioxygen delivery (in hemoglobin and myoglobin), electron transport (in cytochromes), hydrogen peroxide disproportionation (in catalases), organic substrate oxidation (in mono- and dioxygenases) and dioxygen reduction (in terminal oxidases） $\left[6,7,39^{\bullet\bullet}\right]$ . Noncovalent interactions between the porphyrin macrocycle and the peptide sidechains induce secondary structure and enhance the conformational stability of the constructs.
A design derived from the study of natural ferredoxins provided a 16 amino acid ferredoxin maquette that incorporates a low potential $[4\mathrm{Fe}\mathcal{A}\mathrm{S}]^{2+/+}$ cluster $[39^{\bullet\bullet}]$ The ferredoxin maquette provides a minimal system for the study of $[4\mathrm{Fe}\mathbf{\-}4\mathrm{S}]^{2+/+}$ ferredoxin assembly requirements and spectroelectrochemical properties as well as a synthetic module for the construction of larger multicofactor systems. The success of these model systems is yielding a greater understanding of the effects of cofactor incorporation on peptides which is required for the design of enzymatic sites.
## Iron-sulfur clusters
The design of metal (or other cofactor) binding sites in proteins with defined secondary and tertiary structures requires the precise positioning of amino acid residues competent for metal binding (ligands) within the specifically structured environment of a protein. Several groups have succeeded in designing metalloproteins using common, highly stable metal coordination spheres and ligation motifs which may serve to compensate for non-ideal protein designs. The variously designed iron-sulfur proteins provide a case study for comparison of the various.
The difficult challenge of accessing less common metal coordination spheres has been approached by the structurebased redesign of a three helix bundle, CoilSer. A designed ${\mathrm{Hg}}({\mathrm{II}})$ binding site transforms this protein from a metal-favored linear two coordinate geometry to a peptide-favored trigonal three coordinate geometry as the peptide to metal stochiometry increases $\left[45^{\bullet}\right]$ .This pair of symmetric metal coordination environments are controlled by the protein aggregation state. Although promising, similar structure-based design efforts to provide less symmetric metal binding sites, for example blue copper sites, important for biological electron transfer, has not always proven successful [13].
## Functional sites in designed proteins
## Conclusions
Recent publications provide ample evidence that the design and synthesis of novel proteins with enzymatic activity, using a variety of approaches, is progressing well. Chemical methods are producing larger, more complex proteins with covalently attached cofactors, and design rules for conformationally spccific hydrophobic cores are gradually emerging. A reliable, generally applicable methodology (computational or experimental) for the transformation of the multitude of correctly, but not yet uniquely, folded proteins into native-like proteins by systematic mutation, however, is still needed. Additionally, we would benefit from explorations into the consequences of packing defects (cavities) and protein minimization on the dynamic and thermodynamic properties of designed proteins. The rules of cofactor incorporation are being elucidated, and these should meet the challenge of incorporating the full complement of natural cofactors into designed proteins in the foreseeable future. The main challenge remains to build on these significant advances by moving toward the construction of binding sites in a protein that display substrate specificity and have the entatic character intrinsic to chemical catalysis.
